WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.458
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.213 --> 00:00:08.880
NARRATOR: When you
use a Google service,

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.150
your information travels over
the internet between your app

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:14.370
and various points of
internet infrastructure

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:17.100
until it reaches
Google's servers.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:18.900
Encryption helps
prevent attackers

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:21.150
from eavesdropping on
internet connections

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:24.390
and accessing sensitive content,
such as your credentials,

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:26.830
emails, and other personal data.

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:29.400
Do you know how Google
encrypts data to keep it safe?

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:31.360
Stick around, and
we'll show you.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:33.540
First things first,
what is encryption?

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:35.490
Well, it's a
mathematical process

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:38.400
that takes legible data
as input and transforms

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:41.730
it so that it's unreadable
to unauthorized parties.

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:43.690
The encryption algorithm
used is public,

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:46.530
such as the Advanced
Encryption Standard, AES.

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:49.980
But execution depends on a
key, which is kept secret.

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:52.080
To transform it back
to its original form,

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:53.730
you need to employ the key.

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:56.130
Google encrypts your data
when it's saved on a disk

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:57.900
and when stored on backup media.

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:00.150
And this is called
encryption at rest.

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:03.420
It also encrypts data while
it is moving over the internet

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.760
or traveling between
data centers, which is

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.890
called encryption in transit.

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:11.010
Starting with data at rest,
several layers of encryption

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:13.560
are deployed to
protect users' data.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:15.220
Data is split into chunks.

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:18.330
Each chunk is encrypted with
a unique data encryption key.

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:20.250
These keys are
stored with the data

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:24.240
and encrypted inside of Google's
central key management service.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:27.180
Google's key management service
rotates keys at least as

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:28.690
frequently as 90 days.

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:31.320
So in the very unlikely event
that a key were compromised,

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.780
it won't remain
valid for very long.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:37.290
How about a real life example
to put all of this into context?

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:39.690
Let's say a video is
uploaded to Google Drive.

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:43.510
That video will not be stored
entirely in a single server,

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:45.870
but instead will be
broken up into chunks

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:49.140
and then copied for
availability and performance.

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:50.820
Each of these
chunks are encrypted

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:53.220
and have their own
unique identifier.

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:56.550
Each piece is then stored
across multiple servers.

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:59.460
As for data in transit, Google
applies several security

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:02.460
measures to help ensure the
authenticity, integrity,

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:04.830
and privacy of data in transit.

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:06.900
Depending on the connection
that is being made,

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:09.900
Google applies default
protections to data in transit

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:11.580
to a Google data center.

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:14.070
For example,
communications are secured

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:16.140
between the user and
the Google front end

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:19.950
using TLS, which is a
cryptographic protocol.

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:23.190
Data is encrypted and
authenticated in transit at one

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:25.410
or more network
layers when it moves

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:28.560
outside the physical boundaries
of a Google data center.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:30.360
Customers in Google
Cloud, for example,

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:33.310
may have additional requirements
for encryption of data,

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:35.820
especially in highly
regulated industries.

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:37.680
And they have
additional options,

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:40.980
such as managing their
own encryption keys.

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:43.260
And because computers
get better and faster,

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:46.500
it becomes easier to perform
the complicated mathematical

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:49.380
computations needed
to break encryption.

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:51.300
Even the mathematics
behind this process,

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:54.340
known as cryptanalysis,
can improve over time,

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:56.820
making it easier to
break encryption.

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:58.860
And so, to keep pace
with this evolution,

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:02.370
Google has a team of world-class
security engineers tasked

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:04.620
with following,
developing, and improving

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:06.240
encryption technology.

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:09.120
Our engineers take part in
standardization processes

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:12.000
and in maintaining widely
used encryption software,

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.820
such as BoringCrypto
and SSL library

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:18.300
in Chrome Chromium, Android,
and a number of other apps

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:19.800
and programs.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:22.920
We regularly publish research
in the field of encryption

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:26.592
so that everyone can benefit
from this shared knowledge.

00:03:26.592 --> 00:03:28.800
If you want to learn more,
check out the links below.

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:33.020
And stay tuned for more on
discovering datacenters.

